```
### Summary Statistics

The summary statistics above highlight that the fruit group of Apples,Oranges and Bananas had the highest mean and sum, with Citrus fruits having the lowest, significantly less than the other groups analysed.**Generally, North America had the highest amounts of fruit consumption (First globally among Apples,Bananas Oranges and Citrus fruit,and second overall in Other Fruits), whilst Africa and Asia tended to have the lowest (with the exception of Citrus Fruits in Africa).** 

```{r pressure, echo=FALSE}

ggplot(data=data4,aes(x=Apples_Bananas_Oranges,y=Citrus_Fruits,color=Group))+geom_point(size=2)+theme_minimal()+labs(title="Relationship between Apples,Bananas and Oranges and Citrus Fruits")
```

The plot above highlights a relatively strong positive relationship between Apples,Bananas, Oranges and Citrus Fruits, with North America having the highest amount of both fruit groups. *Most geographic groups had a proportionate amount of both fruit groups, with the exception of Africa (who had higher levels of citrus fruit) and Oceania (who had higher levels of Apples, Bananas and Oranges)* 

### Conclusion

Analysis of the data highlights that Apples, Bananas and Oranges are the most common  group, followed by other fruit and citrus fruit.  North America had a consistently high level of all three fruit groups, more than most continents. On the other hand, there was no single continent that consistently low levels of fruits. For example, although Africa had the lowest amounts of Apples, Bananas, Oranges and Other fruits, it had the third highest level of citrus fruit.

As expected, high income countries had the highest amounts of all fruits. This is likely to be attributed to the increased ability to import and perhaps grow a wider range of fruits, however more research is needed to confirm this assumption. Conversely, lower income countries had the lowest amounts of citrus and other fruits, however had a higher amount of Apples, Bananas and Oranges than lower-middle income countries. 